The most common reasons are:

1. **Parent re-rendering** — When the parent component re-renders (for any reason), its children re-render by default. If the child isn't wrapped in `React.memo()`, it will re-render even if props haven't changed.

2. **Object/function props created inline** — Props like `onClick={() => {}}`, `style={{}}`, or `filter={{active: true}}` are new objects every render, so even if they look the same, they fail equality checks:
   ```jsx
   // ❌ New function every render
   <Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />

   // ✅ Stable reference
   const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(), []);
   <Child onClick={handleClick} />
   ```

3. **Child not memoized** — Wrap the child in `React.memo()` to skip re-renders when props are equal:
   ```jsx
   const Child = React.memo(({ name, value }) => {
     console.log('rendered');
     return <div>{name}: {value}</div>;
   });
   ```

4. **Derived values as props** — If props are computed in the parent's render, they're new every time:
   ```jsx
   // ❌ New object every render
   <Child user={{ name: user.name }} />

   // ✅ Stable reference with useMemo
   const userMemo = useMemo(() => ({ name: user.name }), [user.name]);
   <Child user={userMemo} />
   ```

**To diagnose:** Add `console.log('Child rendered')` at the top of your child component, then check whether the parent is re-rendering and whether props are actually identical between renders.

Share your component code if you need help tracking down the specific cause.
